{"id": "q0461", "pool": "easy-chat", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 2.0, "n_chunks": 2, "n_concurrent": 2, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.0528, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": null, "query": "how do you rate strength of tornado", "transcript": "How do you rate strength of tornado?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The strength of a tornado is rated using the Enhanced Fujita Scale, or EF Scale. This scale rates tornadoes from EF0 to EF5 based on the damage they cause. For example, an EF0 is the weakest, with winds around 65 to 85 mph, while an EF5 is the strongest, with winds over 200 mph and capable of leveling houses and throwing cars. Meteorologists assess the damage after a tornado occurs to assign the appropriate rating.", "answer_ms": 2989}
